Git LFS (Large File Storage) keeps large files out of the normal Git history. Git holds a small pointer in place of each file, and the Git host holds the file. A site with thousands of photos needs it: without it, each clone and each data volume holds every version of every photo.

Mica never writes Git LFS settings for you. You choose the files, and Mica follows your choice.

## Set it up

1. Install Git LFS from [git-lfs.com](https://git-lfs.com), and run `git lfs install` once on each computer that uses the repository.
2. Choose the files. In the content repository, run:

```bash
git lfs track "content/**/media/**"
```

3. Commit the `.gitattributes` file that this command writes.

From then on, a new media file goes to Git LFS. Files that are already in the history stay there. To move them, rewrite the history with `git lfs migrate import --include="content/**/media/**"`. That changes every commit, so decide it as a team, and publish all drafts first.

Mica reads `.gitattributes` only. A `.lfsconfig` file in the repository does not change where Mica sends the files.

## The local editor

The local editor uses the Git LFS of your computer.

- `npx mica start` and `npx mica mcp` stop when the content uses Git LFS and Git LFS is not installed or not set up. The message says what to do. Install it, run `git lfs install`, and start again.
- **Publish** sends the large files with `git lfs push` before it pushes the commit. This also works in a checkout that has no LFS hook.
- A file that Git did not download yet is a pointer. The editor says so, and does not show a broken picture. Run `git lfs pull`.

## The self-hosted editor

The image contains Git LFS. You set up nothing in the image.

- Mica uses the repository token for Git LFS too. GitHub, GitLab and Forgejo serve Git LFS with the same token as Git, at the clone URL with `/info/lfs`.
- When your host serves Git LFS at another address, set `MICA_REPOSITORY_LFS_URL`. Mica then gives the token to that host too, so use only a host that you trust.
- The clone downloads only the large files under the content root. Other files stay pointers.
- A sync downloads the large files of the new version before it fast-forwards. If a download fails, Mica keeps the old version and `/ready` has `prefetch_incomplete`. Mica tries again at each sync.
- **Publish** runs `git lfs push` before `git push`. If the host refuses the files ("Authorization error"), the changes are drafts again. Another failure keeps the publication pending, and a sync tries again.
- `docker compose run --rm mica check` reports that the content root uses Git LFS, and that the image has it. It cannot prove write access: a dry run sends no request. The first publication shows a refusal.

## The volume

Each large file is on the data volume twice: in the LFS store (`.git/lfs`) and in the working tree. Old versions stay in the store. Make the volume large enough, and run `docker compose run --rm mica compact` now and then. See [Self-hosting](/docs/self-hosting/).

## The quota of your host

Git LFS storage and bandwidth count against the quota of your Git host, and each clone of the repository by a build counts too. Read the limits of your plan before you move thousands of photos. A host that does not keep within them refuses the push.

## The website build

The build must download the large files. Without that, it sees pointers, and an image fails to build.

```yaml
- uses: actions/checkout@v4
  with:
    lfs: true
```

A host that builds from the repository, such as Netlify or Cloudflare Pages, needs its Git LFS setting turned on. Read its documentation.

## The size of an upload

`MICA_MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES` limits one upload. The default is 25 MB. Raise it when your files are larger. See [Configuration](/docs/configuration/).

## Next

